Below is a **production-ready architecture** you can use for a Spring Boot + Spring AI chatbot that accepts user messages and files — **PDF, CSV, images, responses from other services, etc.** — uses **RAG**, and routes the user request to the correct internal service.

---

## 1. Recommended high-level architecture

Think of your chatbot as **two pipelines**:

```text
                ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                │        User / Client         │
                └──────────────┬──────────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Chat API Controller │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Request Normalizer  │
                    │ text/files/context  │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │  Intent / Router    │
                    │ classify request    │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
          ┌───────────────────┼────────────────────┐
          ▼                   ▼                    ▼
 ┌────────────────┐   ┌────────────────┐   ┌────────────────┐
 │ RAG Answering  │   │ Business Tool  │   │ External API    │
 │ knowledge base │   │ Service A/B/C  │   │ Service Client  │
 └───────┬────────┘   └───────┬────────┘   └───────┬────────┘
         │                    │                    │
         └────────────────────┼────────────────────┘
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Response Composer  │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Final AI Response   │
                    └────────────────────┘
```

And separately:

```text
             ┌─────────────────────────────┐
             │  PDFs / CSV / Images / APIs │
             └──────────────┬──────────────┘
                            ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐
                  │ Ingestion Service │
                  └────────┬─────────┘
                           ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐
                  │ Extract Text/Data │
                  └────────┬─────────┘
                           ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐
                  │ Chunk + Metadata │
                  └────────┬─────────┘
                           ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐
                  │ Embedding Model   │
                  └────────┬─────────┘
                           ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐
                  │ Vector Database   │
                  │ PGVector/Qdrant   │
                  └──────────────────┘
```

Spring AI’s RAG support is built around an ETL flow where `DocumentReader`, `DocumentTransformer`, and `DocumentWriter` produce and store `Document` objects for retrieval; its RAG advisors then use a `VectorStore` during chat requests. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/api/etl-pipeline.html?utm_source=openai))

---

## 2. Core design decision

You should separate these responsibilities:

| Responsibility | Recommended component |
|---|---|
| Chat API | Spring Boot REST/WebSocket controller |
| Conversation memory | Database + Spring AI chat memory advisor |
| Routing | Intent classifier + deterministic rules |
| RAG | Spring AI `QuestionAnswerAdvisor` or `RetrievalAugmentationAdvisor` |
| Tool/service calls | Spring AI tool calling or your own dispatcher |
| Vector DB | PGVector, Qdrant, Redis, Pinecone, Weaviate, etc. |
| File ingestion | Async ingestion workers |
| PDF extraction | Spring AI PDF document reader |
| CSV extraction | Custom `DocumentReader` or Apache Commons CSV |
| Image extraction | OCR/vision model, then store extracted text |
| Observability | Spring Boot Actuator, metrics, traces, logs |

For production, I would **not** let the LLM directly decide everything. Use the LLM for semantic classification, but validate its decision with allowed routes, user permissions, service availability, and confidence thresholds.

---

## 3. Suggested technology stack

For a first production version, I would use:

```text
Backend:        Spring Boot 3.x
AI framework:   Spring AI 2.x
LLM:            OpenAI / Azure OpenAI / Anthropic / local model
Embeddings:     same provider or dedicated embedding model
Vector DB:      PostgreSQL + PGVector
Storage:        S3 / MinIO / Azure Blob for uploaded files
Metadata DB:    PostgreSQL
Queue:          Kafka / RabbitMQ / SQS for ingestion jobs
Cache:          Redis
Observability:  Micrometer + OpenTelemetry + Grafana/Prometheus
```

PGVector is a strong default if you already use PostgreSQL. Spring AI has a PGVector vector store starter and notes that the store needs an `EmbeddingModel` to calculate embeddings for documents. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/api/vectordbs/pgvector.html?utm_source=openai))

Avoid `SimpleVectorStore` for production; Spring AI’s documentation describes it as suitable only for testing or demonstrations. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/api/vectordbs.html?utm_source=openai))

